# `this` of a class as a map of descriptors: the initializers of its fields have stored into it in a body of their own, in front of whatever the constructor stores.
#   function Point() { this.x = 0; }
#   class Props { late = { value: "text" }; constructor() { this.early = { value: 1 }; Object.defineProperties(Point.prototype, this); } }
#   function read(point) { return point.late; }  new Props();  sink(read(new Point()));
# When the scan took any object that the body makes for a map in sight, it named the keys that this body stores ("early") and dropped the record under no key:
# `late` was held never to be defined on Point.prototype, and `typeof new Point().late` was folded to "undefined". SILENTLY: no check of the run time sees it.
